  "response": "I ran `crimes context src/Card.tsx` and read the file plus `tailwind.config.ts`. Context adds two things the scan JSON alone doesn't: **no test file matched or imports `Card.tsx`** (test gap top-quartile, `test_gap: 1.0`), and **blast radius is zero — 0 direct importers, 2 commits in 90d**. So this refactor is cheap and contained: nothing downstream breaks, but nothing catches a regression either.\n\nAll three findings point at line 10's single `style={{...}}` object and its two child `<div>`s — one 25-line component carrying all three charges.\n\nI'm proposing rather than editing: this fixture is scanner input, and rewriting it would move the eval baseline.\n\n## 1. `accessible_interaction_risk` — medium, do this first\n\n`<div onClick>` at line 22 with no `role`, `tabIndex`, or `aria-label`. Keyboard and screen-reader users can't reach it at all. The detector's `fix_shape` is explicit — *use semantic elements; add ARIA only when semantics don't fit* — so `role=\"button\" tabIndex={0}` plus a keydown handler is the fallback, not the fix.\n\nThe wrinkle: `children` is an untyped `React.ReactNode`, so wrapping the whole card in `<button>` risks nested interactive controls (invalid HTML, broken tab order). Two shapes depending on what `Card` actually holds:\n\n**Recommended — stretched primary action.** Card stays a `<div>`; a real `<button>` inside carries the action and covers the card via `::after`. Safe with arbitrary children:\n\n```tsx\nexport function Card({ children, sidebar, onSelect, label }: CardProps) {\n  return (\n    <div className=\"relative isolate ...\">\n      {onSelect ? (\n        <button\n          type=\"button\"\n          onClick={onSelect}\n          className=\"after:absolute after:inset-0 focus-visible:outline-2 ...\"\n        >\n          {label}\n        </button>\n      ) : null}\n      {children}\n    </div>\n  );\n}\n```\n\n**Simpler — if children are guaranteed static** (tighten the prop to `string | ReadonlyArray<string>`), make the shell itself a `<button type=\"button\">` with `text-left`.\n\nEither way, hoist the handler to an optional `onSelect` prop. The current `console.log(\"clicked\")` is debug residue — a card that *looks* clickable but does nothing is exactly the ambiguity that makes an agent extending this file guess wrong. If no `onSelect` is passed, render a plain non-interactive `<div>` with no cursor affordance.\n\n## 2. `design_token_escape` — 9 raw values, 4 kinds\n\n`tailwind.config.ts` already establishes the scale (`brand`, `surface`, `ink`), and none of the nine literals use it. Move every value to a class that reads a token, and add the tokens that genuinely don't exist yet:\n\n| line | raw | token |\n|---|---|---|\n| 17 | `#0a0a0a` | `text-ink` (existing) |\n| 24 | `#333333` | `text-ink-muted` (add) |\n| 27 | `#666666` | `text-ink-subtle` (add) |\n| 18 | `#cccccc` | `border-border` (add) |\n| 20 | `#1a73e8` | `outline-focus` (add) |\n| 19 | `rgb(0, 0, 0)` shadow | `shadow-card` (add to `theme.boxShadow`) |\n| 16 | `#ff00aa` | **needs a design decision** |\n\n`#ff00aa` as a card background isn't a near-miss for any token — it's magenta against a palette of blue/off-white/near-black. My read is it's placeholder junk and should be `bg-surface`; if it's a real accent, it belongs in the config as a named token before it ships. That's the one item here I'd want confirmed rather than assume.\n\nOne trap: `className=\"bg-[#ff00aa]\"` arbitrary-value syntax would satisfy the letter of \"no inline styles\" while still bypassing the palette. The detector may not flag it, but it's the same escape.\n\n## 3. `responsive_fragility` — 3 fixed sizes, no `@media` anywhere\n\n`width: 800` / `720` / `240`, plus `height: 600` and `fontSize: 18`. The `fix_shape` asks for container queries or a fluid scale rather than pixel media queries — right call here, since a card is a component that should respond to *its slot*, not the viewport.\n\n- `width: 800` + `maxWidth: 1024` → `w-fu